    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d237">

                <pb facs="0341" n="273" xml:id="pg273"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">763.72/1554½</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">President Wilson to the
                            Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1915-03-04">4 <hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >March</hi>, 1915</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. Secretary</hi>: The green paper despatch
                    attached hereto<anchor n="47" xml:id="d237-f1"/> seems to me abrupt in
                    expression and also a bit difficult to interpret as it stands. I therefore beg
                    that, in its stead, you will send to Ambassador Page at London Mr. Lansing’s
                    letter to you (also attached),<anchor n="47a" xml:id="d237-f2"/> as I have taken
                    the liberty of altering it.<anchor n="48" xml:id="d237-f3"/> It is both lucid
                    and conveys the matter in just the right tone of inquiry.</p>
                <p>Faithfully Yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>W. W.</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d237-f1" n="47" xml:id="d237-f1-content">
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Supra</hi>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d237-f2" n="47a" xml:id="d237-f2-content">
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Ante</hi>, <ref target="#pg270">p. 270</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d237-f3" n="48" xml:id="d237-f3-content">
                <p>This was done; for the telegram as sent, see <ref target="frus1915Supp#pg_132">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1915, supp., p.
                    132</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
